WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:08.179
Earth is full of perilous places, from the
frozen wastelands of Antarctica, all the way

00:00:08.179 --> 00:00:18.550
to leafy England, let's take a look at the
ten most dangerous places on Earth.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.539
1.

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:23.820
First up is Antarctica.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:30.340
Antarctica is one of the largest land masses
on Earth, covering an enormous 14 million

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.009
square kilometres.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:38.970
Yet it only has around 5,000 temporary residents
and zero permanent residents.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:45.320
Making it the least densely populated country
on Earth, and it's no surprise when you consider

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.110
it's extreme weather conditions.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:54.180
The temperature has been known to go as low
as minus 90 degrees Celsius, couple that with

00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:02.490
astonishing wind speeds of up to 327 kilometres
per hour, and you've got one of the most dangerous

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.300
climates on Earth.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.150
If unprotected, you wouldn't last longer than
an hour in Antarctica.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.150
2.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.480
Next up is the Danakil Desert.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:18.550
Situated in northeast Ethiopia, it has been
named by National Geographic as the cruelest

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.260
place on earth.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:28.480
It's a scary 100,000 square kilometre area
of extremely hot and arid terrain, where daily

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.680
temperatures regularly surpass 50 degrees
Celsius.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:39.970
The desert is also home to the Danakil Depression,
a basin that lies 100 metres below sea level,

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:45.280
which is home to many active volcanoes and
suffers frequent earthquakes.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.400
It's one of the hottest, driest and most violent
places on Earth.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.030
Because of this nobody lives here, except
a few brave salt miners.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:02.799
Oh, and to top it all off, travel to the Danakil
Desert area is strongly discouraged by most

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:07.760
international governments because there's
an extremely high threat of kidnapping in

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.900
the area and border tensions between Ethiopia
and the neighbouring country of Eritrea.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:14.900
3.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.459
Up next is the Guatemala sinkhole.

00:02:18.459 --> 00:02:23.090
Sinkholes are a natural phenomenon caused
by the erosion of the underground rock by

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.379
rainwater.

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:32.730
When too much rock has eroded the ground spectacularly
falls in on itself, creating an enormous sink

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:33.730
hole.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:40.319
These happen all over the world but some places
are more susceptible than others, such as

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:43.189
Florida, Texas and Guatemala.

00:02:43.189 --> 00:02:48.719
One of most devastating sink holes occurred
in 2010 in Guatemala.

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:56.420
When a 20 metre round hole appeared suddenly
in the ground, swallowing up an entire three-story

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:57.420
factory.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.239
The hole it left behind is 30 metres deep.

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:07.359
The sinkhole occurred because of a combination
of recent events, namely the recent Tropical

00:03:07.359 --> 00:03:13.980
Storm Agatha, a nearby volcano erupting and
leaking sewer pipes.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.709
Needless to say, if you visit Guatemala, you
better watch your step.

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:19.709
4.

00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:25.809
Just off the coast of Sao Paolo in Brazil
is an island nicknamed Snake Island.

00:03:25.809 --> 00:03:31.609
The island is riddled with a unique species
of snake called the Golden Lancehead Viper,

00:03:31.609 --> 00:03:35.529
which is one of the most venomous snakes in
the world.

00:03:35.529 --> 00:03:42.110
It is reported there are as many as 5 snakes
in every square metre of the island.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:48.480
The venom of the Golden Lancehead Viper is
so deadly that it melts away human flesh,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:55.120
whilst a powerful neurotoxin quickly shuts
down your central nervous system.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.339
Because the island is so hazardous, access
to the island has been banned by the Brazilian

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:06.089
government, apart from a select few scientists
who monitor the snakes.

00:04:06.089 --> 00:04:07.089
5.

00:04:07.089 --> 00:04:11.370
Lake Nyos is a crater lake in the Northwest
Region of Cameroon.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:15.150
This poisonous lake sits above volcanic earth.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:20.739
Magma below the surface releases massive amounts
of carbon dioxide into the lake, which is

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:24.300
stockpiled in a layer at the bottom of the
lake.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:31.560
When the levels of carbon dioxide at the bottom
of the lake get too high, the lake explodes,

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.240
suffocating anyone and everything in close
proximity to the lake.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.289
The last time this happened was in 1986.

00:04:40.289 --> 00:04:49.120
When the lake suddenly emitted a large cloud
of carbon dioxide that suffocated 1,700 people

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.560
and 3,500 livestock in surrounding towns and
villages.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:59.630
It was the first recorded large scale asphyxiation
caused by a natural event.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:00.630
6.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:07.940
Bolivia is home to the infamous Yungas Road,
more commonly known as the Road of Death.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:13.879
It's an extremely narrow mountain road that
connects La Paz to Coroico.

00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:20.340
It's especially dangerous during rainy season
and has been named by the Inter-American Development

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.599
Bank as the "world's most dangerous road".

00:05:24.599 --> 00:05:29.580
Around 300 travellers are killed on the road
every year.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:35.310
The roadside is scattered with crosses that
mark the spots where travellers have fallen

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.060
to their deaths.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:38.060
7.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:43.820
The North Anatolian Fault is an enormous fault
that runs along most of Turkey.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:48.729
During the past century there have been a
series of devastating earthquakes along the

00:05:48.729 --> 00:05:53.590
North Anatolian Fault of magnitudes above
7.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:58.860
They have been progressively creeping west
along the fault, heading towards Istanbul,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:04.300
which is next on the list to be struck by
the fault-line's devastating earthquakes.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:09.379
The last devastating earthquake was in 1999
in Izmit.

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:18.199
The 7.4 magnitude quake killed 17,480 people
and injured 43,959.

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:26.810
But that could be nothing compared to what
will happen when the earthquake hits the Turkish

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:33.620
capital of Istanbul with a population of 14.2
million people.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:39.810
And it will hit it, this imminent disaster
has been deemed an inevitability, it's just

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.040
a matter of when.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:48.580
Geophysicists estimate the likelihood of Istanbul
being hit by a magnitude 7 or greater earthquake

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.129
in the next 20 years is around 60%.

00:06:52.129 --> 00:06:57.710
Such a disaster would be very bad news for
millions of residents.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:02.210
Making Istanbul one of the most dangerous
places to live.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:03.210
8.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:08.229
Droughts don't often get as much attention
as other, more spectacular natural disasters,

00:07:08.229 --> 00:07:10.900
but they can be one of the most deadly.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:16.669
The Sahel region of Africa, which borders
the Sahara Desert, is one of the most drought

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:18.980
prone places in the world.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:24.990
According to the United Nations an astonishing
100,000 people died due to droughts in the

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:31.280
Sahel region of Africa between 1972 and 1984.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:38.379
Another 750,000 were unable to grow their
crops and had to completely rely on food aid

00:07:38.379 --> 00:07:39.379
instead.

00:07:39.379 --> 00:07:40.379
9.

00:07:40.379 --> 00:07:46.879
Next we go to England, specifically the Alnwick
Poison Gardens in Northumberland.

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:53.580
The garden is specifically designed to securely
grow and protect hundreds of highly poisonous

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.229
and narcotic plants.

00:07:56.229 --> 00:08:02.400
Behind locked cages grows some of the deadliest
plants known to man, such as belladonna, tobacco

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:03.400
and mandrake.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:04.400
10.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:11.159
And finally, and arguably, the most dangerous
place on Earth because of other humans, is

00:08:11.159 --> 00:08:13.060
Honduras.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:19.710
This Central American country has the highest
murder rate in the World, at 90 homicides

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.460
per 100,000 people.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:29.000
And what's more, the second biggest city in
Hoduras, San Pedro Sula has the highest homicide

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:38.140
rate of any city in the world, with 169 murders
occurring for every 100,000 residents.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.100
This is primarily due to drug violence.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:46.450
Ever since Honduras became the key staging
post for cocaine smuggling from South America

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:55.470
to the US, the murder rate has sky rocked,
year on year.

